- in_text_citation: [(Nomoto et al., 2016-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1d5530db8f36fd580af3f270df37d058aab2bab0)
- venue: Nature Communications
- citation count: 82
[method]
The paper used a novelty-based novel object recognition (NOR) paradigm in mice to compare short-term and long-term object memory. Mice received a weak NOR training that supported short-term memory, followed by a behavioural tag training, and neuronal overlap was assessed between the initial weak training and the tag training. Optogenetic experiments were used to manipulate the initial context-ensemble during retrieval, and dopaminergic modulation was tested to determine its role in tagging. This is directly related to the proposed method and research plan because it uses the same object-recognition task to evaluate memory over time and uses engram labeling and optogenetic manipulation to test whether a specific ensemble is required for retrieval, analogous to the plan's c-fos-tTA/AAV9-TRE and ChR2/ArchT dentate gyrus engram approach.
[results]
The reported outcomes were that behavioural tagging transformed NOR-STM into NOR-LTM, that the overlap of neurons active during the initial weak training and the tag training increased during behavioural tag-training, that retrieval of NOR-LTM depended on the initial context-ensemble as shown by optogenetic manipulation, and that dopaminergic modulation was required for the tagging effect. The supplied paper excerpt does not include numerical recognition indices, overlap percentages, group sizes, or statistical p-values, so specific quantitative values and baseline comparisons are not available from the provided text.
[context]
The experimental context was a mouse NOR task with weak-training, tag-training, and control conditions, short-term versus long-term retention, neuronal labeling during defined training windows, optogenetic targeting of the initial context-ensemble, and dopamine-related pharmacological manipulation. These conditions mirror the proposed plan's use of object recognition over time, engram labeling with c-fos-tTA/AAV9-TRE, optogenetic activation/inhibition, and tests of memory preservation, retrieval, and forgetting. The excerpt does not specify exact retention intervals, viral constructs, light parameters, drug doses, or statistical thresholds.
[end related work 1]

