[method]
The paper used a behavioral testing protocol in APP/E3 and APP/E4 mice with Trem2 deletion to evaluate cognitive performance. This is related to the proposed research method because both use mouse behavioral paradigms to assess memory-related performance, although the provided paper text does not specify whether the paper used an object-recognition task, the exact behavioral apparatus, trial timing, or discrimination metrics.
[results]
Trem2 deficiency worsened cognitive performance in APP/E3 and APP/E4 mice. The available text reports this as a qualitative outcome and does not provide quantitative behavioral scores, group sizes, comparisons to baselines, or statistical significance values.
[context]
The experiments were conducted in APP/E3 and APP/E4 amyloid mouse models with APOE isoform-specific comparisons and Trem2 deficiency. Related experimental readouts included plaque compaction and microglial recruitment. The provided excerpt does not specify sample sizes, behavioral task parameters, housing conditions, or statistical tests, limiting direct comparison to the proposed object-memory and forgetting paradigm.
