[start related work 1]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Murthy et al., 2023-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/da036951b18fbe5c0a7c18f2957d107e2f0413d4)
- venue: Frontiers in Behavioral Neuroscience
- citation count: 5
[method]
The paper used cFostTA transgenic mice to drive TRE-dependent expression of an mGRASP fluorescent reporter in neurons activated during experience. Doxycycline-regulated tTA activity was used to gate expression to periods of c-fos activation, and the labeled population was examined after exposure to enriched environment or trace fear conditioning. The method focused on dorsal hippocampal CA1 and CA3, with histological quantification of fluorescent markers and direct comparison to ArcCreER T2-dependent labeling. This is directly relevant to the proposed c-fos-tTA/AAV9-TRE dentate gyrus engram-labeling plan because it tests the same cFos/TRE logic for activity-dependent engram labeling, but the paper used a fluorescent reporter rather than ChR2 or ArchT optogenetic actuators and targeted CA1 rather than the dentate gyrus.
[results]
cFostTA-dependent mGRASP expression after enriched environment or trace fear conditioning labeled a subset of dorsal CA1 cells but did not produce apparent GFP reconstitution. In contrast, ArcCreER T2-dependent mGRASP expression labeled a subset of dorsal CA1 cells and yielded GFP reconstitution, including transsynaptic GFP reconstitution. The ArcCreER T2 system labeled more dorsal CA1 cells than the cFostTA system, and the two systems displayed different expression kinetics. The supplied sections do not provide exact percentages, cell counts, p-values, or effect sizes, so the reported outcomes are qualitative or semi-quantitative rather than fully numerical.
[context]
The experimental context was dorsal hippocampal CA1, with CA3 also examined for expression kinetics, using enriched environment or trace fear conditioning as experience-dependent activation paradigms. The comparison was between cFostTA and ArcCreER T2 systems, with mGRASP expression and GFP reconstitution as the main readouts. The supplied sections do not specify animal numbers, viral titers, doxycycline dosing, injection coordinates, or statistical thresholds. This paper is relevant to the proposed object-recognition forgetting plan because it evaluates cFos/TRE-based engram labeling and doxycycline-gated activity-dependent expression, but it does not include dentate gyrus targeting, optogenetic manipulation, Rac1 modulation, or computational modeling.
[end related work 1]

[start related work 1]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Karaca et al., 2020-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/9f836d570f65f2c10bab20475a9346728eaafc9d)
- venue: bioRxiv
- citation count: 25
[method]
The paper used a spatial object recognition paradigm to measure object memory and a confocal imaging/analysis pipeline to quantify engram reactivation. Mice completed four 6-min training sessions separated by 3-min home-cage intervals: the first session was an empty 50 cm x 50 cm x 50 cm open field with one visual cue, and the next three sessions used two distinct objects, a glass bottle and a metal tower, placed diagonally in the arena. Twenty-four hours after training, mice were tested for 6 min with one object displaced and the other left in its original position. Object exploration was defined as sniffing the object or pointing the nose toward it within 1 cm; leaning on the object without nose orientation was excluded. Memory performance was calculated as a discrimination index: time exploring the displaced object divided by total time exploring both objects, multiplied by 100. For engram reactivation, dentate gyrus tissue was imaged as z-stacks consisting of 3 frames at 2 µm intervals at 20x magnification. Maximum projections were processed in Fiji, and HA-GFP-positive cells and Fos-positive cells were manually marked after background subtraction and thresholding. Reactivation rate was calculated as the number of HA-GFP-positive Fos-positive cells divided by total HA-GFP-positive cells, multiplied by 100. A similarity or overlap index was calculated as the number of HA-GFP-positive Fos-positive cells divided by the union of HA-GFP-positive and Fos-positive cells, multiplied by 100. This method is directly related to the proposed method because it uses an engram marker colocalized with Fos during a memory test and uses object exploration times to compute a discrimination index, analogous to the proposed eYFP/c-Fos colocalization and novel versus familiar object exploration metrics. It also aligns with the research plan's object-recognition model and 24-hour recall assessment.
[results]
