The paper used an environmental enrichment (EC) manipulation compared with standard housing (SC) in mice. Mice were trained on an object recognition memory task, described in the discussion as a Y-maze task, and tested at two inter-stimulus intervals (ISI): 2 h and 24 h. The behavioral readout was object recognition or discrimination performance, comparing exploration of a novel versus familiar object. Brain activation experiments were performed after the presentation and test sessions to assess regional neuronal activation, with particular attention to the hippocampus, infralimbic cortex, and inter-regional correlations. Statistical analysis compared SC and EC groups across ISI and session. This method is directly related to the proposed Experiment 3 because it compares enriched versus standard housing and measures memory persistence, but it does not include the proposed c-fos-tTA/AAV engram labeling, optogenetic engram manipulation, Rac1 pharmacology, reminder or context-only exposures, or computational engram-relevancy modeling.
[results]
SC mice performed the object recognition memory task in a time-dependent manner. With a 2 h ISI, EC did not modify object recognition memory performance relative to SC. With a 24 h ISI, EC prolonged the object recognition memory trace, indicating enhanced memory persistence. EC modulated the neuronal activation profile associated with the presentation session. In EC mice, novel object discrimination after a 24 h ISI was associated with activation of the hippocampus and infralimbic cortex. Hippocampal activity in EC mice was correlated with activity in several other regions. The supplied excerpt does not provide numerical values, sample sizes, effect sizes, or p-values, so quantitative magnitudes and statistical significance cannot be reported.
[context]
