- venue: bioRxiv
- citation count: 25
[method]
The paper used a spatial object recognition (SOR) paradigm in male C57BL/6JRj mice to assess object memory and dentate gyrus engram reactivation. Mice underwent stereotaxic surgery and were placed on a doxycycline-containing diet. Three weeks after surgery and after 5 days of handling, mice completed four 6-minute SOR training sessions separated by 3-minute home-cage intervals. The first session was an empty 50-cm square open field with a visual cue on one wall and was also used to score open-field behavior, including time in a central zone covering 32% of the arena, center entries, and total distance traveled. The next three training sessions included two diagonally placed distinct objects, a glass bottle and a metal tower. Twenty-four hours after training, mice were tested for 6 minutes with one object displaced and the other left in its original position. Object exploration was defined as sniffing the object or pointing the nose toward it within 1 cm; leaning on the object without nose direction was not counted. Memory performance was calculated as the time spent exploring the displaced object divided by the total time spent exploring both objects, multiplied by 100. Dentate gyrus tissue was imaged as three-frame z-stacks at 2-µm intervals using 20x confocal microscopy, and HA-GFP-positive engram cells and Fos-positive cells were manually marked after background subtraction and thresholding. Reactivation rate was calculated as the proportion of HA-GFP-positive cells that were also Fos-positive, and similarity index was calculated from the overlap between HA-GFP-positive and Fos-positive populations. This method relates to the proposed environmental enrichment experiment by providing the object-memory behavioral readout and engram-reactivation metrics that would be used to compare enriched versus standard housing conditions. It also aligns with the broader research plan’s use of object recognition and c-Fos-based engram labeling, although the paper did not test environmental enrichment, longer forgetting intervals, or optogenetic manipulation.
[results]
No quantitative behavioral or imaging outcomes are provided in the supplied excerpt; the Results section is empty. The only available indication is the section title stating that cognitively impaired aged mice exhibit impaired recall-induced neuronal ensemble, but no numerical values, group comparisons, effect sizes, or statistical significance are reported.
[context]
Experiments used 8-10-week-old or 18-20-month-old male C57BL/6JRj mice individually housed on a 12-hour light/dark cycle with ad libitum food and water. After stereotaxic surgery, regular food was replaced with doxycycline-containing diet at 40 mg/kg. Behavioral testing was performed during the light phase, 3 weeks after surgery and after 5 days of handling. The arena was a 50 cm × 50 cm × 50 cm black square open field with a visual cue on one wall, and the central zone was 32% of the arena. The memory test was conducted 24 hours after training. Imaging was performed on a Nikon A1R confocal microscope using NIS-Elements software or a Leica SP8 confocal microscope using LAS X software. Maximum projections were processed in Fiji, and cells were manually marked after background subtraction and signal thresholding. Statistical analysis was blinded to experimental conditions. Data were first tested for normality using the Shapiro-Wilk test. For normally distributed data, one-way ANOVA followed by Tukey’s multiple comparisons was used for three-group comparisons; for non-normally distributed data, Kruskal-Wallis followed by Dunn’s multiple comparisons was used. For two dependent data sets, paired t-test or Wilcoxon matched-pairs signed rank test was used depending on normality. Correlation analysis used Pearson or Spearman tests depending on distribution. Analyses were performed in GraphPad Prism version 8. Aged mice were classified as cognitively impaired if their SOR memory performance was lower than one standard deviation below the mean performance of the young group. Sample sizes are not specified in the provided excerpt.
