Processing method: To assess recognition memory over time and investigate conditions under which a memory can be preserved, retrieved, or forgotten, we will utilize a mouse model of object memory based on an object recognition task that exploits the natural tendency of mice to explore novel stimuli.
Processing method: To establish the temporal dynamics of object memory forgetting, we will test mice at 24 hours, 1 week, 2 weeks, and 3 weeks after acquisition training and measure both behavioral performance and engram reactivation patterns.
Processing method: To test whether dentate gyrus engrams are necessary and sufficient for memory retrieval, we will use optogenetic approaches with ChR2 for activation and ArchT for inhibition of identified engram cells, inhibiting them during recall tests and activating them after natural forgetting has occurred.
Processing method: To investigate experience-dependent modulation of forgetting, we will house mice in enriched environments containing additional objects and stimuli, then assess forgetting rates and engram reactivation patterns compared to standard housing conditions.
Processing method: To quantify engram reactivation and memory performance throughout the experiments, we will measure colocalization of original engram markers (eYFP) with c-Fos expression during memory tests and discrimination indices based on novel versus familiar object exploration times.
Processing method: To label and manipulate engram cells within the dentate gyrus, we will employ c-fos-tTA transgenic mice combined with AAV9-TRE viral vectors, using the c-fos promoter to selectively label neurons active during learning and doxycycline control to restrict labeling to specific time windows.
Processing method: To test whether context-only exposure accelerates forgetting by updating the original memory engram, we will repeatedly expose mice to the training context without objects and test whether optogenetic inhibition of the engram during these exposures prevents the accelerated forgetting.
Processing method: To test whether forgotten memories can be recovered, we will expose mice to brief reminder sessions consisting of the original training environment and objects, then test whether this can restore access to forgotten memories and increase engram reactivation.
