[method]
The paper describes high-resolution time-lapse imaging of dendritic synaptic structures, coupled with fluorescent biosensors and actuators, to monitor and manipulate synapses in defined dendritic domains before and after learning. It also summarizes behavioral fear-conditioning and extinction paradigms used to assess spine turnover and clustering in cortical pyramidal neurons. This is related to the proposed method because both aim to link localized dendritic structural changes to memory performance, but the paper's approach is not limited to c-fos-labeled engram cells and does not directly compare successful recall with forgetting in an object-recognition task.
[results]
The paper reports qualitative outcomes rather than quantitative spine density or volume values. It states that localized synaptic dynamics in dendrites correlated with learning, memory performance, and synapse clustering in CCR5 knockout animals. It also summarizes findings that fear conditioning increased spine elimination in frontal association cortex, extinction produced newly formed spines near previously eliminated spines, and auditory fear conditioning increased spine formation with new spines clustering within dendritic branch segments and being spatially segregated by auditory cue. No statistical tests, effect sizes, or direct recall-versus-forgetting comparisons are provided.
