  "suggested_action": "The most important improvement is to make the computational model explicitly error-driven by adding a prediction-error term that updates an engram-relevancy variable, and to derive testable predictions for Experiments 1 through 7, such as how prediction-error magnitude during context-only exposure should change c-Fos/eYFP colocalization and discrimination index. The next priority is to adopt the related work's recall and engram-selectivity metrics as formal model outputs and compare them to the plan's behavioral and molecular measures, including baseline models without error-driven forgetting, fixed engram weights, Hebbian plasticity, and inhibition-only plasticity. The model should also incorporate inhibitory plasticity and competing engrams, because [(Tomé et al., 2022-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c6bf46daf6f943ef16e0678f4c0fceacb1b9c11d) found inhibition crucial for selectivity, and this would help explain whether context-only exposure accelerates forgetting through engram competition or relevancy decay. To improve reproducibility and statistical rigor, the plan should specify network size, synaptic weight initialization, plasticity rates, simulation duration, MLE likelihood function, confidence intervals, and significance tests, and it should pre-register quantitative thresholds for recovery after ArchT inhibition or ChR2 activation. Finally, the model should be adapted from fear to object memory by including dentate gyrus-specific constraints, object-novelty exploration dynamics, and Rac1 as a modulatory gate on plasticity, so that the computational predictions are directly testable in the proposed mouse experiments.",
